{
  "configuration_id": "config_043",
  "reason": "Evidence: The dominant signal is clearly RNA (0.485 vs 0.208 for image-only), and the best configuration so far (config_019: multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, weighted, acc=0.639) uses only a linear PCA encoder for RNA. The aggregation (avgpool > transformer) and fusion (weighted > concat) choices are now well-established. The largest unexplored dimension that could move performance is the RNA encoder quality, since RNA drives most of the signal. Question tested: Does replacing PCA with scgpt (a pretrained single-cell foundation model capturing non-linear transcriptomic structure) improve on the current best in the otherwise-identical multimodal/multiprofile/avgpool/weighted setting? Why this is the best next experiment: It isolates exactly one variable (RNA encoder) from the best-performing configuration, so any performance change is attributable to the encoder choice. If scgpt helps, it opens a high-impact axis for further gains; if it does not, we learn PCA is sufficient and can redirect budget toward image encoder or other factors. This is more informative than testing a better image encoder (given image-only is only 0.208) or re-testing aggregation/fusion combinations already shown to be suboptimal."
}

